    "youth schools",
    "kindergartens",
    "vocational schools",
    "Central Honshu map"
   ],
   "start_page": 226,
   "summary": "This subfile forms the final portion of the SCAP Civil Information and Education Section's roster of educational institutions for Ishikawa Prefecture. It catalogues private male, female, and co-educational youth schools, kindergartens, and miscellaneous schools, as well as vocational and youth schools operated under the Ministries of Transportation, Communications, Home Affairs, and Welfare. Each entry specifies school name, location, founding date, enrollment by sex, and full-time and part-time teaching personnel as of April 30 and November 30, 1946. The subfile concludes with a reference map of Central Honshu indicating prefectural capitals, cities over 100,000 population, and higher educational institutions.",
   "title": "Roster of Japanese Educational Institutions - Ishikawa Prefecture",
   "to": ""
  },
  {
   "about": "A SCAP comprehensive report and statistical directory cataloguing educational institutions across Saga Prefecture by level, type, and administrative jurisdiction as of 1946.",
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1948-09-25",
   "doc_type": "special report",
   "end_page": 296,
   "from": "Educational Research Unit, Research Branch, Analysis and Research Division, Civil Information and Education Section, General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers",
   "keywords": [
    "Saga Prefecture",
    "SCAP Civil Information and Education Section",
    "educational statistics",
    "school roster",
    "higher education",
    "secondary education",
    "elementary education",
    "youth schools"
   ],
   "start_page": 235,
   "summary": "This special report documents educational institutions throughout Saga Prefecture based on data gathered between December 1947 and September 1948 for benchmark dates in April and November 1946. An extensive introduction examines Japanese educational record-keeping deficiencies, defines school categories and control jurisdictions (national, public, and private), and discusses structural transitions toward the 6-3-3-4 system. Parts One and Two present detailed statistical summary tables analyzing school counts, student enrollments, and faculty counts by gender and control authority. Part Three provides an itemized institutional roster recording founding dates, locations, enrollments, and teacher numbers for national higher and normal schools, prefectural secondary and vocational schools, municipal schools, and village or town youth and elementary schools.",
   "title": "Roster of Japanese Educational Institutions and Statistical Tables - Part 33. Saga Prefecture (Special Report Serial Number R-302-E-L-26-33)",
   "to": ""
  }
